T11.01|A revenue model explains what customers pay for and how receipts scale; a cost structure explains which resources are consumed as activity changes. Product sales, subscriptions, usage fees, advertising and transaction commissions create different margins and cash timing.~~Revenue is not always gross customer spending: an intermediary may recognise only its fee under relevant principal-agent criteria. Analyse contribution, retention and capital needs alongside headline growth.|Transaction revenue: Payment per sale or event.;;Recurring revenue: Subscription or ongoing service fees.;;Usage revenue: Amount varies with consumption.;;Advertising or commission: Monetise audience or facilitate another party's sale.|A marketplace processes ₹100 lakh customer orders and retains a 10% fee. Assume it is an agent and bears ₹6 operating costs.~~Gross transaction value is ₹100.;;Recognised fee revenue under the assumption is ₹10.;;Operating profit before other items is ₹10 − ₹6 = ₹4.~~Customer spending and the platform's revenue differ.~~Contractual role, refunds and incentives determine actual recognition and economics.|Map payer, service and pricing unit.;;Separate gross activity from retained revenue.;;Classify cost drivers and funding needs.
T11.02|Fixed costs remain unchanged within a relevant activity range; variable costs change with volume; semi-variable costs combine both. Some costs step upward when capacity thresholds are crossed. Classifying cost behaviour helps forecast contribution and break-even.~~A cost is not permanently fixed or variable across all horizons. Rent can be fixed this year and adjustable later; labour may be partly flexible or constrained by minimum staffing.|Fixed cost: Unchanged within a stated range.;;Variable cost: Changes with output or activity.;;Mixed or step cost: Combines a base with usage or capacity thresholds.|Monthly fixed cost is ₹100 and variable cost ₹2 per unit. Produce 50 units.~~Total variable cost = ₹100.;;Total cost = ₹100 fixed + ₹100 variable = ₹200.;;At 100 units, total cost is ₹300 and cost per unit falls from ₹4 to ₹3.~~Higher utilisation spreads fixed cost without reducing variable cost per unit.~~A capacity expansion could add a new fixed-cost step and change the comparison.|State the time horizon and range.;;Identify the activity driver.;;Model capacity steps and avoidable costs.
T11.03|Contribution margin is revenue less costs that vary with the activity under the chosen definition. It first covers fixed costs; only the remainder is operating profit. Break-even volume equals fixed cost divided by unit contribution when prices and unit variable costs are constant.~~Gross profit and contribution need not match because accounting cost-of-sales and managerial cost behaviour use different classifications. Multi-product break-even depends on sales mix.|Unit contribution: Price minus unit variable cost.;;Contribution ratio: Contribution/revenue.;;Break-even point: Activity at which contribution equals fixed cost.|Price is ₹10 per unit, variable cost ₹6 and fixed cost ₹100.~~Unit contribution = ₹4.;;Break-even volume = ₹100/₹4 = 25 units.;;At 40 units, profit = 40 × ₹4 − ₹100 = ₹60.~~Sales below 25 units produce a loss under these assumptions.~~Discounts, capacity steps or changing product mix require an updated model.|Classify costs by behaviour.;;Calculate contribution before fixed cost.;;Test mix, price and capacity assumptions.
T11.04|Price-volume-mix analysis separates revenue changes caused by price, quantity and product composition. The decomposition depends on the order and convention used; interaction terms must be handled consistently. It helps explain whether growth came from more units, better pricing or a richer mix.~~Revenue effects do not translate one-for-one into profit. Products have different contribution and variable costs, and discounts can increase volume while reducing total margin.|Price effect: Changed selling price on a defined quantity base.;;Volume effect: Changed units at a defined price base.;;Mix effect: Changed proportions of products with different prices or margins.|One product sells 100 units at ₹10, then 120 at ₹11. Use base-price volume effect followed by price on current volume.~~Revenue rises from ₹1,000 to ₹1,320.;;Volume effect = 20 × ₹10 = ₹200.;;Price effect = 120 × ₹1 = ₹120; total change ₹320.~~A declared convention reconciles the full change.~~With multiple products, isolate mix and consider contribution, not just revenue.|State the decomposition convention.;;Reconcile effects to total change.;;Translate revenue drivers into cost and profit effects.
T11.05|Capacity utilisation measures output relative to usable capacity. Fixed-cost absorption can improve as utilisation rises, creating operating leverage. However, bottlenecks, overtime, maintenance and quality problems can make costs nonlinear near the limit.~~Unused capacity can be intentional resilience or evidence of poor demand. The relevant denominator is practical usable capacity, not a theoretical maximum that ignores downtime.|Design capacity: Theoretical technical limit.;;Practical capacity: Feasible output after normal constraints.;;Utilisation: Actual output/relevant capacity.|Practical monthly capacity is 100 units and fixed costs ₹300. Output increases from 50 to 75 units.~~Utilisation rises from 50% to 75%.;;Fixed cost per unit falls from ₹6 to ₹4.;;Assess whether variable costs or service quality change at the higher load.~~Spreading fixed costs improves unit economics within this range.~~A new shift or machine can create another cost step; the relationship is not unlimited.|Identify practical bottlenecks.;;Separate fixed absorption from variable efficiency.;;Test cost and quality near capacity limits.
T11.06|Economies of scale reduce average cost as one activity expands; economies of scope reduce total cost from producing different activities together. Scale benefits can come from spreading fixed costs, purchasing or specialised processes. Scope benefits can come from shared distribution, data or infrastructure.~~Greater size can also create diseconomies through coordination, complexity and weaker service. Claimed savings need an actual resource mechanism rather than a vague assertion that larger is better.|Scale economy: Lower unit cost with greater output.;;Scope economy: Shared resources lower combined cost.;;Diseconomy: Complexity raises costs or reduces effectiveness.|Product A costs ₹100 lakh alone and B ₹80 alone. A shared delivery system makes combined cost ₹150.~~Separate cost totals ₹180.;;Scope saving = ₹180 − ₹150 = ₹30.;;Check integration and shared-service costs before concluding the benefit persists.~~The saving arises from joint production, not merely more units of one product.~~Savings can disappear if service requirements conflict or shared resources become bottlenecks.|Identify the shared resource.;;Compare feasible standalone and combined costs.;;Include complexity and execution costs.
T11.07|Customer acquisition cost divides relevant acquisition spending by customers acquired under a stated cohort and attribution definition. Fully loaded CAC includes appropriate sales, marketing and onboarding resources; paid-channel CAC may include a narrower set. These measures should not be mixed.~~Use genuinely acquired customers, not leads or downloads, and allow for sales-cycle lag. Low CAC is valuable only when retained contribution and customer quality justify the spending.|Paid-channel CAC: Attributed paid acquisition spend/new customers.;;Blended CAC: Relevant total spend/all acquired customers.;;Fully loaded CAC: Includes specified staff and support resources.|Spend ₹1,200 acquiring 100 paying customers, while a further ₹300 sales-support cost is relevant.~~Narrow CAC = ₹1,200/100 = ₹12.;;Fully loaded CAC = ₹1,500/100 = ₹15.;;Compare each only with a lifetime-value measure of compatible scope.~~The definition changes the result materially.~~Attribution, cohort timing and customers who would arrive organically affect causal interpretation.|Define spend and customer eligibility.;;Match costs with acquisition cohorts.;;Evaluate retained contribution rather than acquisition volume alone.
T11.08|Customer lifetime value estimates the present value of future customer contribution under retention, spending, service-cost and discount assumptions. Revenue-based lifetime measures are not the same as contribution value. Acquisition cost should be subtracted or compared consistently.~~A simple contribution/churn shortcut assumes stable behaviour and restrictive timing. Cohort models are preferable when customers expand, decline, reactivate or incur varying service costs.|Revenue lifetime: Total expected customer sales.;;Contribution LTV: Value after relevant ongoing costs.;;Net customer value: LTV less acquisition and other included costs.|Assume a customer produces ₹100 annual contribution for three years, then leaves; discount rate is 10% and CAC ₹150.~~Contribution PV = 100/1.1 + 100/1.1² + 100/1.1³ = ₹248.69.;;Net value after CAC = ₹98.69.;;Stress shorter retention or lower contribution.~~Positive revenue alone would not establish positive net customer value.~~The forecast must include actual service, payment and retention economics.|Model cohorts and cash timing.;;Use contribution rather than unqualified revenue.;;Test retention and acquisition assumptions.
T11.09|Retention measures how much customer activity remains; churn measures loss under a specified definition. Customer-count retention differs from revenue retention because surviving customers can expand or contract. Cohort analysis follows customers acquired together to distinguish age effects from growth in new acquisitions.~~Do not mix annual and monthly churn or infer long-run behaviour from a short early cohort. Net revenue retention can exceed 100% while some customers still leave.|Logo retention: Customers retained/customer starting count.;;Gross revenue retention: Existing revenue after losses and contraction, excluding expansion.;;Net revenue retention: Includes expansion within the starting cohort.|A cohort begins with 100 customers paying ₹100 each. Ten leave; retained customers collectively add ₹2,000 spending.~~Logo retention = 90%.;;Assuming no contraction, gross revenue retention = ₹9,000/₹10,000 = 90%.;;Net revenue retention = ₹11,000/₹10,000 = 110%.~~Revenue can grow while customer count falls.~~Expansion concentration and price increases should be separated from durable usage growth.|Define population and period.;;Separate loss, contraction and expansion.;;Compare cohorts rather than only total business growth.
T11.10|Customer and product profitability allocates relevant revenue and costs to a defined unit. Direct contribution differs from profit after shared-cost allocation. A customer can buy high revenue while consuming costly returns, support or delivery.~~Allocated overhead is not automatically avoidable. Decisions to discontinue should compare the cash consequences of removal, including stranded fixed costs, rather than relying only on an accounting allocation.|Direct contribution: Revenue less attributable incremental costs.;;Fully allocated profit: Includes a stated share of common cost.;;Avoidable profit effect: Costs and benefits changing if the unit is removed.|A customer generates ₹100 revenue, ₹70 direct cost and ₹35 allocated overhead. Assume only ₹10 of overhead is avoidable if the customer leaves.~~Allocated profit is −₹5.;;Current direct contribution is ₹30.;;Dropping the customer loses ₹30 contribution but saves ₹10, reducing total profit by ₹20.~~An allocated loss does not automatically justify discontinuation.~~Capacity constraints or better alternative customers can change the decision.|Distinguish direct, allocated and avoidable costs.;;Assess service and return drivers.;;Compare actual alternatives and capacity use.
T11.11|CAC payback estimates the time needed for customer contribution to recover acquisition spending. Use contribution after relevant ongoing costs rather than revenue. Discounted payback additionally reflects time value; both measures can ignore value after the recovery date.~~Fast payback improves financing resilience but does not establish high lifetime value or long retention. A customer can repay CAC quickly and then leave, while a slower cohort may still create more total value.|Simple payback: Undiscounted cumulative contribution recovers cost.;;Discounted payback: Recovery using present-valued contribution.;;Post-acquisition contribution: Earnings after specified acquisition allocation.|CAC is ₹120 and monthly contribution ₹20, constant from the first month.~~Simple payback = 120/20 = 6 months.;;At only ₹10 contribution, payback becomes 12 months.;;If customers typically leave after four months, they do not recover the assumed CAC.~~Payback must fit observed retention and cost definitions.~~Discounting and uneven onboarding costs require a dated cumulative schedule.|Use contribution consistently.;;Match cohort timing and retention.;;Assess liquidity and total customer value together.
T11.12|Working-capital intensity measures operating-cycle funds tied up per unit of activity; capital intensity measures longer-lived resources needed for capacity. Growth in both can consume cash even when margins are stable. Asset-light businesses can still have substantial receivable or customer-acquisition funding needs.~~Compare incremental needs, not only historical average ratios. New capacity may arrive in large steps and remain underutilised during ramp-up.|Working-capital intensity: Operating cycle investment/activity.;;Fixed-capital intensity: Long-lived operating investment/activity.;;Incremental intensity: New capital needed for additional activity.|Revenue grows ₹100 lakh. Assume incremental operating working capital 20% of revenue and new equipment ₹30.~~Additional working capital = ₹20.;;Combined investment is ₹20 + ₹30 = ₹50.;;Compare resulting cash contribution with this funding need.~~Growth can require ₹50 cash before all benefits arrive.~~Supplier credit and customer advances may reduce the cycle requirement but add obligations.|Map each funding driver.;;Use incremental and seasonal assumptions.;;Connect growth, capital utilisation and returns.
T11.13|Network effects occur when participation improves value for other users, such as more buyers attracting sellers. They can support retention or pricing but also require incentives, moderation, reliability and sufficient local density. Network effects are not synonymous with economies of scale.~~Financial analysis should show how participation changes contribution, acquisition cost or retention. A growing network subsidised indefinitely may fail to become economically self-supporting.|Same-side effect: Users benefit from more users on their side.;;Cross-side effect: One participant group benefits another.;;Negative effect: Congestion, poor quality or competition reduces user value.|A marketplace adds sellers and attracts buyers, but pays ₹20 lakh incentives to earn ₹12 extra contribution.~~Incremental current contribution net of incentive is −₹8.;;Estimate whether later retention or density changes improve economics.;;Test the required improvement rather than treating user growth as proof.~~A network mechanism needs a credible cash-economic bridge.~~Benefits may be geographically limited or vulnerable to users joining several competing platforms.|Identify who benefits and why.;;Measure retention and contribution effects.;;Include incentives, quality and competition.
T11.14|Pricing changes contribution as well as demand. A discount reduces unit margin and must generate enough incremental volume or future benefit to compensate. Revenue management balances price, availability, capacity and customer willingness to pay under relevant constraints.~~Price elasticity is context-dependent; historical promotions can be confounded by seasonality and selection. Test contribution after refunds, fulfilment and acquisition costs rather than simply observing higher sales.|List and realised price: Posted amount versus actual net receipt.;;Discount strategy: Price reduction for a defined purpose.;;Dynamic or segmented pricing: Prices vary with demand or customer context.|Price is ₹10 and variable cost ₹6, giving ₹4 contribution. A ₹1 discount reduces contribution to ₹3.~~Contribution falls 25% per unit.;;To preserve total contribution, volume must rise by 4/3 − 1 ≈ 33.33%.;;Check capacity and promotion costs before concluding the discount pays.~~A 10% price cut requires more than 10% extra volume here.~~Customer retention and cannibalisation can alter the longer-term result.|Use realised net price.;;Calculate contribution break-even volume.;;Test demand, capacity and customer behaviour.
T11.15|Driver-based analysis connects operational quantities with statements: customers × activity × price produces revenue; resources and unit costs produce expenses; timing and capacity produce working capital and assets. It makes forecasts inspectable rather than assuming every line grows with sales.~~Drivers should be independent enough to avoid double counting. If both customer growth and total-volume growth represent the same expansion, multiplying them can inflate the forecast.|Volume driver: Units, customers or transactions.;;Price and mix driver: Net receipt per activity.;;Resource driver: Staff, materials, capacity and funding.|Assume 100 customers each buy 2 units at ₹10, with variable cost ₹6 per unit and fixed cost ₹300.~~Revenue = 100 × 2 × ₹10 = ₹2,000.;;Variable cost = 200 × ₹6 = ₹1,200.;;Profit before other items = ₹2,000 − ₹1,200 − ₹300 = ₹500.~~Operational assumptions reconcile to financial results.~~Collections, stock and capital spending need separate drivers to forecast cash.|Define nonoverlapping drivers.;;Reconcile units and financial lines.;;Connect revenue and margin to cash and capital needs.
